  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 301 AM EDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\n=====Friday - Midweek=====\n\nThe weak cold frontal boundary moving through the area should slowly \nclear our SE communities though remain close enough for increased \ncloud cover Friday morning in the SE. Further towards the NW in \nsouthern IN and along the Ohio River, clearing skies may allow for \nfog formation as dew points will remain elevated in the upper 60s at \nleast. Further towards the south, there may be enough remnant \nmoisture for some morning-daytime showers and perhaps a rumble of \nthunder. PoPs remain low for these areas as there still remains \nuncertainty on if an impulse of activity along the Appalachians can \nform to create more shower coverage for KY. Otherwise, temperatures \nremain warm low to mid 80s with light southerly winds.\n\nFor the weekend, the synoptic scale flow features a deep digging \ntrough over the eastern CONUS, further supported by strong ridging \nupstream over the mountain west of CONUS. With a high at the \nsurface, conditions should at least initially be dry Saturday. \nDepending on a shortwave expected to move into the eastern US, \nspotty to scattered convection may return in the afternoon. However, \nthere is a growing trend to keep the trough axis too far east to \nfeature any really impactful storms in our CWA. For now, low \nafternoon PoPs will remain Saturday night into Sunday.\n\nOnce the shortwave passes, slightly and much drier air should filter \ninto the lower Ohio Valley for Sunday, making conditions feel much \nless muggy. Sunday should mostly be dry as well. Highs reach the mid \nto upper 80s Saturday to the low 80s Sunday. As for early next week, \ntroughing over the eastern seaboard of the US seems to persist and \nwe'll be in between that and a ridge to our west. Being on the \nperiphery of continuous NW flow, any little disturbance traveling \nalong this flow will continue to keep low PoPs in place. Highs are \ncooler to start, gradually warming as the week progress from the low \n80s to upper 80s by Wednesday.",
